## ps-classic | Classic experiments | psych | 0 | - | ps-bias
story 5 | Milgram: ordinary people, lethal shocks
- 1961: volunteers were told to give increasing electric shocks to a "learner" who got answers wrong. The shocks were fake; the screams were acted.
- In the best-known version, about 65% went all the way to the maximum 450 volts when calmly told to continue.
- *Later analyses note many participants doubted the shocks were real.* The core finding still shook psychology.
> Asch showed people deny their own eyes

story 4 | Asch's line experiment
- 1951: participants judged which of three lines matched a reference line. The answer was obvious.
- Planted actors all gave the same wrong answer first.
- *About three-quarters of participants conformed at least once.* But most answers were still correct.
> The Stanford prison experiment is shakier than its fame

myth 4 F | The Stanford Prison Experiment proved that ordinary people turn cruel when given power.
= Recordings and interviews later showed guards were coached toward toughness, and some prisoners said they were acting.
= It was never published as a peer-reviewed study in the usual way and can't support strong conclusions.
> The marshmallow test has been reinterpreted

explain 4 | The marshmallow test, revisited
- 1970s: children who waited longer for a second marshmallow later did better on various life measures.
- A larger 2018 replication found the link shrank a lot once family background and early ability were accounted for.
- *Waiting may partly reflect whether a child has reason to trust that the reward will come.*
> The bystander effect

explain 4 | The bystander effect
- People are less likely to help when others are present: responsibility diffuses.
- The famous 1964 Kitty Genovese story ("38 witnesses did nothing") was heavily exaggerated by the press.
- *A 2019 study of real CCTV found someone intervened in about 90% of public conflicts.*
> Next: how habits work

## ps-habit | Habits and learning | psych | 0 | - | ps-classic
explain 4 | The habit loop
- Cue → routine → reward. Repeat enough and the routine becomes automatic.
- Habits shift control from deliberate goal-directed brain systems toward automatic ones.
- *That's why you can drive home and not remember the journey.*
> How long does a habit take?

myth 3 F | It takes 21 days to form a habit.
= The 21-day figure traces back loosely to a 1960 self-help book about plastic surgery patients.
= A 2009 study found new habits took anywhere from 18 to 254 days, with a median around 66.
> Variable rewards are the strongest

explain 4 | Variable rewards: the slot-machine schedule
- Skinner found behaviour rewarded on an unpredictable schedule is the hardest to extinguish.
- Pigeons pecked hundreds of times for an occasional, random reward.
- *It's the engine behind slot machines, social feeds, and the mystery drops in this app.*
> Dopamine is about wanting, not liking

explain 4 | Dopamine signals surprise, not pleasure
- Dopamine neurons fire most for unexpected rewards, less for expected ones, and dip when an expected reward doesn't come.
- This "reward prediction error" is how the brain learns what to expect.
- *It's the same maths as reinforcement learning in AI.*
> Next: motivation

## ps-motiv | Motivation | psych | 0 | - | ps-habit
explain 4 | Rewards can kill interest
- 1973: children who loved drawing were promised a reward for it. Later they drew less in free time than children who weren't.
- Expected external rewards can crowd out intrinsic motivation (the overjustification effect).
- *Unexpected rewards don't seem to do the same damage.*
> The goal gradient

explain 3 | The goal gradient effect
- People speed up as they get closer to a goal: rats run faster near the food, coffee-card holders buy faster near the free drink.
- A card with 12 stamps and 2 pre-filled gets completed faster than a card with 10 and none filled.
- *Same work remaining; the head start changes behaviour.*
> Why your progress bar matters

quiz 3 | Self-determination theory says intrinsic motivation needs three things. Which is NOT one of them?
* Financial reward
o Autonomy
o Competence
o Relatedness
= Deci and Ryan: autonomy (choice), competence (mastery), relatedness (connection).
> Flow is the sweet spot of challenge

explain 4 | Flow
- Csikszentmihalyi: deep absorption when challenge matches skill.
- Too easy: boredom. Too hard: anxiety. Just right: time disappears.
- *Good games and good teachers keep you right on that edge.*
> Next: sleep

## ps-sleep | Sleep | psych | 0 | - | ps-motiv
explain 3 | Sleep cycles last about 90 minutes
- Light sleep, deep sleep, then REM (when most vivid dreaming happens).
- Deep sleep dominates early in the night, REM later.
- *Waking from deep sleep is what makes you groggy.*
> Sleep cleans the brain

explain 4 | The glymphatic system
- 2013 mouse studies found the spaces between brain cells widen during sleep, letting fluid flush out waste.
- Waste products include amyloid-beta, linked to Alzheimer's disease.
- *Research in humans is ongoing, but it's one more reason sleep matters.*
> The record for staying awake

story 4 | Randy Gardner stayed awake for 11 days
- 1964: a 17-year-old stayed awake for about 264 hours for a science fair.
- He developed mood changes, memory problems and hallucinations, but recovered after sleeping.
- *Guinness stopped accepting the record because it's dangerous.*
> Teenagers' clocks really do shift later

real 3 | Why teenagers can't get up
- Puberty shifts the body clock later: melatonin is released later in the evening.
- Some schools that moved start times later saw better attendance and sleep.
- *It's biology, not just laziness.*
> Next: how perception fools you

## ps-perc | Perception and illusions | psych | 0 | - | ps-sleep
story 5 | The dress: blue and black, or white and gold?
- 2015: a photo of a dress split the internet.
- People's brains made different assumptions about the lighting: shadow (so it's white/gold) or warm light (so it's blue/black).
- *The real dress was blue and black.*
> You have a blind spot in each eye

explain 3 | Your blind spot
- Where the optic nerve leaves the retina there are no light receptors.
- Your brain fills in the gap using the surroundings, so you never notice.
- *Close one eye and you can make a dot on a page vanish.*
> Change blindness

story 4 | Swapping a person mid-conversation
- 1998: an experimenter asked strangers for directions. Mid-conversation, workers carrying a door passed between them, and a different person took the asker's place.
- About half of the strangers didn't notice.
- *We see far less than we think we do.*
> The gorilla in the room

quiz 3 | In the famous "invisible gorilla" study, what fraction of viewers missed the gorilla?
* About half
o About 5%
o About 90%
o Nobody
= Viewers counting basketball passes often failed to see a person in a gorilla suit walk through (Simons & Chabris, 1999).
> Next: social psychology

## ps-soc | Social psychology | psych | 0 | - | ps-perc
explain 4 | The fundamental attribution error
- Someone cuts you up in traffic: they're a jerk. You cut someone up: you were late for something important.
- We explain others' behaviour by character, our own by circumstance.
- *Cultures vary in how strongly they do this.*
> The spotlight effect

explain 3 | The spotlight effect
- Students wearing an embarrassing T-shirt guessed about half of the room noticed. Only about a quarter did.
- People think others notice them far more than they do.
- *Everyone else is busy worrying about themselves.*
> Dunbar's number

explain 4 | Dunbar's number
- Robin Dunbar proposed humans can maintain about 150 stable relationships, based on primate brain sizes.
- Layers: about 5 intimate, 15 close, 50 good friends, 150 meaningful contacts.
- *Critics question how precise the number is, but the layered pattern shows up in many datasets.*
> Next: the replication crisis

## ps-rep | The replication crisis | psych | 0 | - | ps-soc
story 5 | In 2015, most famous psychology results failed to replicate
- The Open Science Collaboration re-ran 100 published psychology studies.
- About 36% of the replications found statistically significant results, compared with 97% of the originals.
- *Effect sizes were on average about half as large.*
> Power posing

myth 4 F | Standing in a "power pose" for two minutes raises testosterone and lowers stress hormones.
= The original 2010 hormone results didn't replicate in larger studies.
= One co-author later publicly said she no longer believed the effect was real.
> Why it happened

explain 4 | How false findings get published
- Small samples, many measured outcomes, and flexible analysis let random noise look significant.
- Journals favoured surprising positive results.
- *Fixes: pre-registration, larger samples, sharing data.*
> Ego depletion took a hit too

explain 4 | Willpower as a battery: the ego depletion debate
- Studies suggested self-control uses up a limited resource, even that sugar drinks restored it.
- A large 2016 multi-lab replication found an effect close to zero.
- *The debate continues, but the simple "battery" story is in doubt.*
> Next: emotions and stress

## ps-emo | Emotion and stress | psych | 0 | - | ps-rep
explain 3 | Stress isn't always bad
- Short bursts of stress sharpen focus and memory (the Yerkes–Dodson inverted U).
- Chronic stress, lasting weeks or months, harms sleep, immunity and memory.
- *Some studies suggest that viewing stress as useful can change how the body responds.*
> Naming an emotion calms it

explain 4 | Affect labelling
- Putting feelings into words ("I feel anxious") reduces amygdala activity in brain scans.
- It's one reason journaling and talking help.
- *"Name it to tame it."*
> Happiness and money

quiz 4 | What did a large 2021 study find about income and happiness?
* Well-being kept rising with income, with no plateau at $75,000
o Happiness stops rising above $75,000
o Money has no link to happiness
o Richer people are less happy
= Killingsworth (2021) found experienced well-being kept rising with income.
= A 2023 follow-up with Kahneman found the plateau held only for the unhappiest group.
> Next: how to actually study

## ps-study | Study techniques that work | psych | 0 | - | ps-emo
explain 4 | The best study techniques, ranked
- A 2013 review (Dunlosky et al.) rated practice testing and spaced practice as the most effective.
- Highlighting, re-reading and summarising rated low.
- *Feeling productive and being productive are different things.*
> Interleaving beats blocking

explain 4 | Interleave your practice
- Mixing different problem types feels harder than doing one type in a block.
- But it trains you to choose the method, which is what exams test.
- *In one study, interleaved maths practice led to much higher test scores a week later.*
? Why does interleaving help exam performance? :: It trains you to identify which method a problem needs, not just to execute one method
> Explaining to someone else

explain 3 | The Feynman technique
- Explain the idea in simple words as if teaching a child.
- Where you get stuck or use jargon, you've found a gap. Go back and learn it.
- *Teaching is one of the best ways to learn.*
> Pomodoro and breaks

quiz 3 | Which study habit is LEAST supported by evidence?
* Studying in your preferred "learning style" (visual, auditory…)
o Spacing study sessions out
o Testing yourself
o Sleeping after learning
= Studies matching teaching to learning styles haven't found benefits.
= The other three are among the best-supported techniques.
> Psychology track complete
